# Aequitas — TLS reverse proxy for Contabo1 (the primary validator).
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
# ----------------
# The mobile app and the dapp talk to https://aequitas.digital (see
# lib/config.ts in the app repo: API_BASE, RPC_URL, WEBAPP). Two things in the
# chain code also require real TLS on a real hostname:
#
#   1. isAllowedPeerURL (x/humanity/keeper/sync_blocks.go) accepts http:// only
#      for literal public IPs. For a HOSTNAME it requires https:// — that check
#      exists to stop DNS rebinding.
#   2. The browser pages use getUserMedia (camera) and wallet APIs, which
#      browsers gate on a secure context.
#
# STATE AS FOUND (2026-08-14, measured)
# -------------------------------------
# Contabo1 already runs a Caddy container named `proof-caddy` serving exactly
# one site:
#
#     proof1.aequitas.digital { reverse_proxy proof-server:3000 }
#
# That works: proof1.aequitas.digital resolves to 173.249.37.118 and returns
# the proof server's /health. The chain node itself was NOT served over TLS at
# all — only on :8080 plain HTTP.
#
# Meanwhile aequitas.digital resolved to 69.46.46.73, a DIFFERENT host running
# a node at height 96 with 0 humans and no peers, while the real chain on this
# box was past 3.74 million. So every app install was talking to an empty
# chain. Pointing the domain here is what fixes that, and this file is what
# makes the domain serve something once it does.
#
# DEPLOY
# ------
# This box runs Caddy in a container, not as a systemd service, so install it
# where that container reads its config and reload:
#
#     docker cp deploy/Caddyfile proof-caddy:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#     docker exec proof-caddy caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#
# Certificates are obtained automatically via Let's Encrypt (HTTP-01 on port
# 80, already open and already reaching this container). A hostname only gets
# a certificate once its DNS actually points here — so apply the DNS change
# first, then reload.

# The proof server. Unchanged from what is already running — keep it, so
# reloading this file never takes the existing site down.
proof1.aequitas.digital {
	reverse_proxy proof-server:3000
}

# The chain node: API, RPC and the browser pages the app links to.
aequitas.digital, www.aequitas.digital {
	# The node listens on plain HTTP inside the docker network. Both this
	# container and the node are on `aequitas-net`, so the container name
	# resolves directly — no host port hop, and the node's :8080 does not need
	# to be public at all.
	#
	# X-Forwarded-For: Caddy sets it, and the node's clientIP()
	# (x/humanity/keeper/register.go) only trusts that header when the TCP peer
	# is private/loopback — i.e. exactly this proxy. That pairing is what makes
	# the per-IP rate limits count real client IPs instead of filing every
	# request under one address. Do not widen it.
	reverse_proxy aequitas-node:8080

	# Compression is deliberately NOT enabled. The node already spends
	# measurable CPU on gzip for /rpc responses (~3.5% in the 2026-07-27 CPU
	# profile); a second pass in front of it costs CPU on a box whose ceiling
	# is already CPU-bound, for no gain.

	# Security headers are set per-route by the node itself (X-Frame-Options,
	# X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy and a per-page CSP — see
	# handleUI/handleDapp/handleLanding in x/humanity/keeper/api.go). Not
	# duplicated here on purpose: a blanket header at the proxy would override
	# the per-page CSPs, which differ by page by design.

	log {
		output file /var/log/caddy/aequitas.log {
			roll_size 50MiB
			roll_keep 5
		}
	}
}

# NOTE — the libp2p listener (port 4001) is NOT proxied and must not be. It
# speaks its own transport with its own encryption and peer-ID authentication,
# not HTTP, and has to stay directly reachable on 4001/tcp — that is what the
# bootstrap multiaddrs in x/humanity/keeper/p2p.go point at.
# Verify with:  nc -vz <ip> 4001
